## Why This Is Asked

Real-world fintech data assistants service multiple tenants; the router must enforce per-tenant policies, guard against data leakage, and handle conflicting signals and latency constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Tenant-aware policy retrieval and governance
- Guardrails and data-redaction
- Edge-case handling: conflicting signals, missing policy, leakage

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-router sketch
async function routePrompt(prompt, tenantId){
  const policy = await fetchPolicy(tenantId);
  if(!policy) throw new Error('Missing policy');
  if(!policy.allow(prompt)) throw new Error('Action not allowed');

  const intent = analyzeIntent(prompt);
  const module = selectModule(intent, policy);
  const plan = generatePlan(module, intent);

  return { module, plan, payload: buildPayload(module, prompt) };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This evaluates your ability to design pragmatic tooling that ensures prompt reliability without requiring complex NLP infrastructure, focusing on version control and output consistency.

## Key Concepts
- Semantic prompt versioning and management
- Output determinism validation through stable hashing
- Delta scoring for output comparison
- Lightweight testing frameworks
- Performance observability

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal prototype showing two prompts and hash comparison
const crypto = require('crypto');

function hash(obj) {
  return crypto.createHash('sha256').update(JSON.stringify(obj)).digest('hex');
}

function calculateDelta(output1, output2) {
  const hash1 = hash(output1);
  const hash2 = hash(output2);
  return hash1 === hash2 ? 0 : 1;
}

const prompts = {
  v1: 'Get monthly signup counts by region',
  v2: 'Calculate monthly user signups organized by geographic region'
};

function mockLLM(prompt) {
  return { data: `Response for: ${prompt}`, timestamp: Date.now() };
}

function selectVersion() {
  const outputs = {
    v1: mockLLM(prompts.v1),
    v2: mockLLM(prompts.v2)
  };

  const delta = calculateDelta(outputs.v1, outputs.v2);
  const selectedVersion = delta === 0 ? 'v1' : 'v2';

  return {
    selectedVersion,
    payload: outputs[selectedVersion],
    deltaScore: delta
  };
}

console.log(selectVersion());
```

## Implementation Notes
The system uses SHA-256 hashing for deterministic output comparison and a simple delta score to measure consistency between versions.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical prompt versioning and drift handling with concrete routing decisions for enterprise data assistants.

## Key Concepts
- Drift detection via keyword overlap scoring
- Baseline prompt management with patch generation
- Three-tier routing: Intent-Detector, Safety-Guard, Response-Composer
- Signal tracking: intent, data_sensitivity, version_delta
- Edge case handling: renamed intents, missing baselines

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code for drift detection and routing
function routePrompt(newPrompt, baselinePrompt) {
  const overlap = calculateKeywordOverlap(newPrompt, baselinePrompt);
  const hasSafetyIssues = detectSafetyKeywords(newPrompt);

  if (hasSafetyIssues) return 'Safety-Guard';
  if (overlap < 0.6) return 'Intent-Detector';
  return 'Response-Composer';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you calibrate the overlap threshold for different use cases?
- What approach would you use to store patches for audit trails?
- How would you handle version conflicts when multiple teams modify prompts simultaneously?

